#Printing elements of a list using a while loop.

_list = [1, 'Harry', False, 'This ', 'Rohan', 'Suresh', 5]# 
i = 0 # This line initializes the variable i with the value 0. This variable will be used as an index to access elements of the list. Starting with 0 allows us to access the first element of the list, which is at index 0. As we increment i in the loop, we will be able to access subsequent elements of the list until we reach the end.  
while i < len(_list): # This line starts a while loop that will continue to execute as long as the condition i < len(_list) is true. The function len(_list) returns the number of elements in the list, which is 7 in this case. The loop will run as long as i is less than 7, allowing us to access each element of the list using its index. Once i reaches 7, the loop will stop executing, preventing us from trying to access an index that does not exist in the list.
    print(_list[i])# This line prints the current element of the list that is being accessed using the index i. Each time the loop runs, it will output the element at index i, starting from index 0 (the first element) and incrementing i in each iteration to access the next element until it reaches the end of the list.
    i += 1 #    This line increments the value of i by 1 in each iteration of the loop. This is crucial to ensure that the loop eventually terminates. If we did not increment i, it would remain at 0 indefinitely, resulting in an infinite loop. By incrementing i, we allow it to eventually reach the value of len(_list), which will cause the loop to stop executing when we have accessed all elements of the list.